Q: Authentication failing from Azure app service to Azure Key Vault even though access policies are set A: <p>Your personal credentials succeed only via the Visual Studio because that's the first method in the chain <code>(EnvironmentCredential → ManagedIdentityCredential → VisualStudioCredential → AzureCliCredential, etc.)</code> that resolves to you. This is expected your Azure AD identity needs to be in the vault's access policy with the right permissions. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/azure.identity.defaultazurecredential?view=azure-dotnet" rel="nofollow noreferrer">chain of credential providers</a></p>
<p>while excluding Visual studio you are supposed to use something else <code>(e.g. Azure CLI)</code></p>
<pre><code>az login  
</code></pre>
<p>then ensure AzureCliCredential is enabled in the chain, or use it directly.</p>
<pre><code>var client = new CertificateClient(new Uri(&quot;...&quot;), new AzureCliCredential());
</code></pre>
<p>I would normally be explicit about which credentials to use per environment instead of relying on the full <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> chain</p>
<pre><code>public X509Certificate2 GetCertFromKeyVault()
{
    var isProduction = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT&quot;) != &quot;Development&quot;;

    TokenCredential credential = isProduction
        ? new ManagedIdentityCredential()       // App Service uses system-assigned MI
        : new VisualStudioCredential();         // Local dev uses your VS credentials

    var client = new CertificateClient(
        new Uri(&quot;https://keyvaultname.vault.azure.net/&quot;),
        credential);

    return client.DownloadCertificate(&quot;certname&quot;).Value;
}
</code></pre>

Q: &#39;&lt;&#39; is an invalid start of a value. Path: $ | LineNumber: 0 | BytePositionInLine: 0 A: <p>If you have your JavaScript model as:</p>
<pre><code>const person = {
    name: 'John Doe',
    age: 30,
    isEmployed: true
};
</code></pre>
<p>Using AJAX you should pass the data like:</p>
<pre><code> data: JSON.stringify(person)
</code></pre>
<p>Then your Controller Should expect:</p>
<pre><code>public async Task&lt;JsonResult&gt; UpdateProfile([FromBody] JsonObject jsonModel)
    {
       try
       {
        YourModel model = new YourModel();
         model.name = jsonModel.name;

         await _data.UpdatePerson(model);

       }
       catch(Exception ex)
       {
         (CODE)
       }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>It works for me. You can always try other ways that may be better or not, but this one works fine for me.</p>

Q: Selenium Webdriver - how to get the value in span tag A: <p>You can use</p>

<pre><code> driver.findElement(By.id("email-display")).getText();
 driver.findElement(By.xpath("//span[@id='email-display']")).getText();
 driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("span#email-display")).getText();
</code></pre>

<p>If that element is in new window or in frame, then switch to that window / frame, then perform the operations.</p>
